      "isAnalyzable": true,
      "displayName": "Unannotated Intron Junctions",
      "fullName": "GeneQuestions.GenesByIntronJunctions",
      "description": "This search identifies genes that contain, or are flanked by, unannotated introns \u2013 defined as RNAseq reads that map to discontinuous regions on the same DNA strand as a gene, span canonical splice donor & acceptor motifs, and are not reflected in the reference annotation for that gene.  These introns may suggest incorrect gene model annotation or alternatively-spliced transcripts, and should be carefully reviewed in the genome browser, in the context of additional evidence (abundance in various samples, frequency of splicing relative to flanking exon sequences, etc).  Changes to the gene model can then be made using the Apollo annotation system, if warranted.  Note that clicking on individual introns in the genome browser displays a table providing much relevant information.\n<p/>\n<b>Search Parameters:</b>\n<p/>\n<b>\"Consider 5' & 3' Flanking Sequence up to (bp)\"</b> expands the query beyond annotated gene model boundaries, enabling the identification of up- or down-stream introns that may have been missed in the reference annotation.\n<p/>\n<b>\"Minimum number of unique reads >=\"</b> controls the number of intron-spanning reads (ISRs) that must support an unannotated intron before the gene is returned by the search.  Introns with low abundance relative to the total number of reads mapping to a region may not be biologically significant. \n<p/>\n<b>\"Percent of most abundant intron\"</b> defines unannotated introns in comparison to the most abundant intron (MAI) associated with that gene.  Introns mistakenly omitted from the annotated gene model are likely to be as abundant as correctly annotated introns; alternative splice variants may be observed at ~20-80%, but you may not be interested in rare 'typographical errors' made by the splicing machinery.",
      "defaultAttributes": [
